WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 60: The New Harmony Utopian Experiment: Robert Owen's Rational Society

1
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.840
[Rebecca] Welcome back to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:05.840 --> 00:00:07.120
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

3
00:00:07.120 --> 00:00:15.280
[Rebecca] In 1825, along the fertile banks of the Wabash River in southern Indiana, an extraordinary social experiment was launched.

4
00:00:15.280 --> 00:00:26.800
[Rebecca] Robert Owen, a wealthy Welsh textile manufacturer and social reformer, purchased an entire frontier town of 30,000 acres for 125,000.

5
00:00:26.800 --> 00:00:31.360
[Rebecca] His goal was nothing less than the total transformation of human civilization.

6
00:00:31.360 --> 00:00:40.400
[Rebecca] A cooperative utopian community where private property was abolished, education was universal, and crime and poverty were engineered out of existence.

7
00:00:40.400 --> 00:00:48.480
[Rebecca] It attracted America's greatest scientists, philosophers, and dreamers, along with a fair share of eccentrics and opportunists.

8
00:00:48.480 --> 00:00:55.200
[Rebecca] George brings us the visionary, chaotic story of New Harmony.

9
00:00:55.200 --> 00:00:58.480
[George] Robert Owen was not an Ivory Tower theorist.

10
00:00:58.480 --> 00:01:03.760
[George] He was one of the wealthiest and most successful cotton manufacturers in the British Empire.

11
00:01:03.760 --> 00:01:17.280
[George] At his colossal cotton mills in New Lanark, Scotland, Owen had astonished European royalty and industrial tycoons by demonstrating that humane treatment of factory workers actually increased industrial productivity.

12
00:01:17.280 --> 00:01:36.720
[George] In an era when British industrialists worked seven-year-old children for 14 hours a day in airless factories, Owen banned child labor under the age of 10, introduced an eight hour workday, built clean workers' housing, opened free public daycares, and established the world's first modern infant school.

13
00:01:36.720 --> 00:01:45.760
[George] Under Owen's paternal management, New Lanark generated immense commercial profits while practically eliminating crime, drunkenness, and illiteracy.

14
00:01:45.760 --> 00:01:49.680
[George] But Owen's ambition reached far beyond factory reform.

15
00:01:49.680 --> 00:01:54.400
[George] He believed that human character was entirely formed by social environment.

16
00:01:54.400 --> 00:02:06.840
[George] If you placed human beings in an environment of shared cooperative labor, rational scientific education, and communal brotherhood, the evils of greed, competition and poverty would wither away forever.

17
00:02:07.160 --> 00:02:19.400
[George] Convinced that the entrenched class hierarchies of classbound Europe were too rigid for total social regeneration, Owen looked across the Atlantic toward the vast, virgin wilderness of the American frontier.

18
00:02:19.400 --> 00:02:31.080
[George] European monarchs and philosophers visited New Lanark in astonishment, marveling at the clean gardens and laughing school children who sang in multipart harmony rather than toiling in cold dust.

19
00:02:31.080 --> 00:02:45.160
[George] Owen's experiments in Scotland had drawn international acclaim from social reformers and European royalty, who marveled at how clean working environments and enlightened schooling had eradicated crime and poverty among the new Lanark millworkers.

20
00:02:45.160 --> 00:02:51.960
[George] In 1824, the opportunity arrived in the wilderness of Posey County, southwestern Indiana.

21
00:02:51.960 --> 00:03:07.800
[George] For a decade, a devout sect of German Christian communal separatists known as the Harmony Society, led by the charismatic mystic George Rapp, had carved a prosperous agricultural and manufacturing town out of the dense river bottomlands along the Wabash River.

22
00:03:07.800 --> 00:03:22.280
[George] The Rappites had cleared thousands of acres of timber, planted award winning vineyards and apple orchards, built sturdy brick communal halls and houses, and established woolen and flour mills that shipped goods down the Mississippi to New Orleans.

23
00:03:22.280 --> 00:03:31.160
[George] Desiring to move back east to Pennsylvania, Father Rapp put the entire town and its 30,000 acres of prime land up for sale.

24
00:03:31.160 --> 00:03:43.400
[George] When Robert Owen sailed into New York in November 1824, he purchased the town lock, stock, and barrel for $125,000, equivalent to tens of millions today.

25
00:03:43.400 --> 00:03:57.920
[George] Renaming the settlement New Harmony, Owen delivered an address before a joint session of the United States Congress in Washington, attended by President James Monroe, President elect John Quincy Adams, and Supreme Court justices.

26
00:03:57.920 --> 00:04:10.560
[George] Owen proclaimed that New Harmony would inaugurate a new moral world based on universal cooperation, shared property, and rational enlightenment, inviting all people of good will to join him on the Wabash.

27
00:04:10.560 --> 00:04:21.120
[George] The Indiana legislature welcomed Owen with open arms, viewing the arrival of European capital and scientific minds as an extraordinary boon for the young frontier state.

28
00:04:21.120 --> 00:04:32.880
[George] Early settlers faced immense hardships carving farms and homesteads from the dense eastern woodlands, battling disease, inclement weather, and frontier isolation with unyielding resolve.

29
00:04:32.880 --> 00:04:47.920
[George] Father George Rapp's celibate harmonists had spent a decade draining swamps, planting extensive vineyards, and constructing sturdy two-story brick dormitories, leaving behind a turnkey frontier paradise that astonished Owen when he stepped ashore.

30
00:04:47.920 --> 00:04:56.800
[George] In response to Owen's magnetic public invitations, over 800 people poured into New Harmony during the spring and summer of 1825.

31
00:04:56.800 --> 00:05:03.280
[George] But the crown jewel of the community arrived in January 1826 aboard the keelboat Philanthropist.

32
00:05:03.280 --> 00:05:08.320
[George] Forever celebrated in American intellectual history as the boatload of knowledge.

33
00:05:08.320 --> 00:05:18.640
[George] Descending the frozen Ohio River and polling up the Wabash, the vessel carried the most luminous galaxy of scientific and artistic talent ever assembled in the early republic.

34
00:05:18.640 --> 00:05:47.160
[George] Aboard were William McClure, the wealthy Scottish merchant and father of American geology who served as president of the Philadelphia Academy of Natural Sciences, Thomas Say, the renowned pioneer entomologist and concologist, Charles Alexander Lessouer, the brilliant French naturalist and botanical artist, Gerard Troust, a master mineralogist, and Madame Marie Duclos Fretagot, a pioneering Swiss progressive educator.

35
00:05:47.160 --> 00:06:02.920
[George] These scholars established research laboratories, public museums, and advanced printing presses in the Indiana woods, transforming New Harmony almost overnight into the leading center for natural history research and progressive education west of the Allegheny Mountains.

36
00:06:02.920 --> 00:06:15.640
[George] As the philanthropist moored at the New Harmony Landing, crowds cheered from the bluffs, welcoming crates of delicate brass microscopes, geological hammers, and rare scientific folios into the wilderness.

37
00:06:15.640 --> 00:06:26.600
[George] The spirit of American innovation was evident in every workshop and meeting hall, where practical mechanics and visionary thinkers sought solutions to the most challenging obstacles of their era.

38
00:06:26.600 --> 00:06:41.080
[George] The intellectual luminaries aboard the keelboat philanthropist spent their evenings debating paleontology, entomology, and progressive pedagogical models by candlelight, viewing the Ohio River as their voyage to an earthly paradise.

39
00:06:41.080 --> 00:06:54.920
[George] On July 4, 1826, the 50th anniversary of American independence, Robert Owen stood in the New Harmony Community Hall and delivered his famous radical declaration of mental independence.

40
00:06:54.920 --> 00:07:10.760
[George] Owen proclaimed that while the American Revolution had emancipated citizens from political tyranny, humanity remained enslaved by what he termed a monstrous trinity of evils, private property, organized sectarian religion, and commercialized marriage.

41
00:07:10.760 --> 00:07:15.120
[George] Owen announced the complete abolition of private ownership within new harmony.

42
00:07:14.840 --> 00:07:20.640
[George] All houses, land, livestock, and tools became the common property of the community.

43
00:07:20.880 --> 00:07:29.520
[George] Every member was expected to contribute labor according to their skills and draw supplies from the communal storehouse without monetary transactions.

44
00:07:29.520 --> 00:07:34.480
[George] Education was completely restructured under William McClure's Pestilasian method.

45
00:07:34.480 --> 00:07:48.160
[George] Rote memorization and physical corporal punishment were abolished in favor of hands-on vocational learning, natural history exploration in the forests, and daily instruction in singing, drawing, and scientific mechanics.

46
00:07:48.160 --> 00:07:55.760
[George] Boys and girls received identical academic and industrial training, an equality unprecedented in the Western world.

47
00:07:55.760 --> 00:08:06.640
[George] Children spent mornings exploring limestone bluffs along the river, collecting fossil crinoids and pressing wildflowers under the guidance of world-renowned European naturalists.

48
00:08:06.640 --> 00:08:18.880
[George] News of these extraordinary events traveled by telegraph and steam train across the nation, captivating readers in major eastern cities and inspiring future generations of pioneers and inventors.

49
00:08:18.880 --> 00:08:32.880
[George] Owen's passionate antisectarian rhetoric electrified free thinkers across the United States, but deeply alarmed Orthodox frontier clergy who viewed his rejection of traditional dogma as an existential threat to Christian civilization.

50
00:08:32.880 --> 00:08:40.160
[George] Yet beneath the lofty philosophical declarations, practical reality began tearing the utopian fabric apart.

51
00:08:40.160 --> 00:08:46.400
[George] Owen had invited the world to new harmony without establishing any screening process for prospective members.

52
00:08:46.400 --> 00:09:03.240
[George] While devoted scientists, honest craftsmen, and hardworking farmers arrived, they were heavily outnumbered by excentric visionaries, theoretical philosophers, and indolent opportunists who had no desire to clear ditches, milk cows or hoe corn under the humid summer sun.

53
00:09:03.720 --> 00:09:09.080
[George] Intense disputes erupted between the manual laborers and the educated intellectuals.

54
00:09:09.080 --> 00:09:21.640
[George] A blacksmith who swung a heavy sledgehammer for ten hours a day bitterly resented that a resident philosopher received the exact same communal flower ration for sitting under an oak tree discussing universal harmony.

55
00:09:21.640 --> 00:09:25.160
[George] Furthermore, cultural divisions proved intractable.

56
00:09:25.160 --> 00:09:37.000
[George] English liberals clashed with conservative backwoods homesteaders over religious observance, while William McClure and Robert Owen feuded bitterly over administrative authority and educational funds.

57
00:09:37.000 --> 00:09:47.400
[George] Within 18 months, factions splintered off into separate competing subcommunities, fencing off gardens and accusing one another of selfishness and authoritarian rule.

58
00:09:47.400 --> 00:09:57.320
[George] The communal kitchen struggled to feed hundreds of residents, as theoretical debates over the nature of human virtue took precedence over planting cabbage and butchering hogs.

59
00:09:57.320 --> 00:10:08.520
[George] The enduring lessons of this historic chapter continue to resonate across American culture, reminding us of the courage, sacrifice, and vision of those who shaped the destiny of our nation.

60
00:10:08.520 --> 00:10:21.960
[George] While intellectuals and philosophers delivered inspiring lectures in the village hall, the communal gardens grew choked with weeds because few theoretical thinkers possessed the practical stamina to operate frontier plows in the summer heat.

61
00:10:21.960 --> 00:10:35.720
[George] By the spring of 1827, having lost over four fifths of his vast personal fortune subsidizing the community's chronic agricultural deficits, Robert Owen conceded that the communal experiment was unsustainable.

62
00:10:35.720 --> 00:10:51.600
[George] In a poignant, farewell address to the residents in May 1827, Owen acknowledged with dignified humility that human character, conditioned for centuries by competitive societies, could not be transformed overnight simply by abolishing property titles.

63
00:10:51.600 --> 00:11:04.640
[George] Owen deeded portions of the town lands to his sons and business partners, transferred educational institutions to William McClure, and returned to Britain to pursue cooperative labor unions and consumer cooperatives.

64
00:11:04.640 --> 00:11:13.680
[George] The communal property was partitioned into private holdings, and the town transitioned into an ordinary, peaceful Midwestern agricultural village.

65
00:11:13.680 --> 00:11:19.840
[George] Yet remarkably, there was no violence, no criminal corruption, and no lingering bitterness.

66
00:11:19.840 --> 00:11:28.640
[George] Owen's grand experiment dissolved not with a fiery clash of arms, but with a quiet, polite recognition of the complexities of human nature.

67
00:11:28.640 --> 00:11:39.040
[George] Owen parted with his fellow reformers on cordial terms, emphasizing that future generations would build upon the educational and social principles pioneered on the Wabash.

68
00:11:39.040 --> 00:11:54.000
[George] Recognizing that human nature resisted complete economic uniformity, Owen gracefully accepted the limitations of communal living, deeding portions of the town to scientific societies and educational institutes that continued his philanthropic mission.

69
00:11:54.000 --> 00:12:04.000
[George] Though Owen's utopian communal structure survived barely two years, the intellectual seeds planted at New Harmony transformed American culture for generations.

70
00:12:04.000 --> 00:12:10.560
[George] Owen's four sons remained in Indiana, becoming prominent statesmen, scientists, and educators.

71
00:12:10.560 --> 00:12:25.120
[George] His eldest son, Robert Dale Owen, was elected to Congress, where he authored the landmark legislation creating the Smithsonian Institution and drafted Indiana's progressive constitution granting married women legal property rights.

72
00:12:25.120 --> 00:12:35.160
[George] David Dale Owen became the first United States geologist, directing federal geological surveys across the Upper Midwest from his New Harmony Laboratory.

73
00:12:35.160 --> 00:12:45.400
[George] The town pioneered the first free public library system in the United States, established America's first public kindergarten, and founded the Working Men's Institute.

74
00:12:45.400 --> 00:12:53.320
[George] Today, the quiet, treelined streets of New Harmony, Indiana are preserved as a national historic landmark district.

75
00:12:53.320 --> 00:13:09.800
[George] Visitors from around the world walk past the historic brick halls and the modernist roofless church, reflecting upon a community of dreamers who dared to imagine a world guided not by selfish competition, but by the highest ideals of reason, science, and human brotherhood.

76
00:13:09.800 --> 00:13:20.520
[George] Today, the tranquil gardens of New Harmony stand as an immortal testament to the audacious dreamers who believed that human society could be guided by universal harmony and truth.

77
00:13:20.520 --> 00:13:35.240
[George] The educational and scientific legacy of New Harmony permanently enriched American society, fostering the creation of the United States Geological Survey and establishing the prototype for public school libraries across the Midwest.

78
00:13:35.560 --> 00:13:46.360
[Rebecca] Robert Owen's New Harmony proved that while perfect utopias may elude human reach, the pursuit of knowledge, equality, and compassion leaves a lasting mark on history.

79
00:13:46.360 --> 00:13:47.640
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

80
00:13:47.640 --> 00:14:02.600
[Rebecca] Join us next week as we head to the brickyard of Speedway, Indiana, for the very first running of the Indianapolis 500.

